---
/**
 * Redesign option — Flows → Journeys → Playbooks case study.
 *
 * Reframes the live /work/beyond-the-task.astro (currently a heavily
 * placeholder-flagged page — 9+ "[ADD: ...]" evidence markers, no real
 * metrics, explicit "to be verified" language on AI Playbooks status) into
 * the brief's product-evolution story. The product decision described here
 * (AI proposes, admin decides) is the one piece of real content in the
 * source page; everything else that was a placeholder there is marked
 * ContentNeeded here too, rather than invented fresh.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CaseHero from "../../components/CaseHero.astro";
import DecisionBlock from "../../components/DecisionBlock.astro";
import ContentNeeded from "../../components/ContentNeeded.astro";
import ConfidentialityNotice from "../../components/ConfidentialityNotice.astro";
import SignalChain from "../../components/SignalChain.astro";
---

<Base title="Flows → Journeys → Playbooks — Kishore Sudhamalla">
  <CaseHero
    eyebrow="Case study 02 · Darwinbox"
    title="From Flows to Journeys to Playbooks."
    lede="Rethinking employee experiences for an AI-native HCM platform — a product evolution story, not a single redesign. Journeys is released and in use by clients; AI Playbooks is concept and prototype work, with current rollout status still to be verified."
    meta={[
      { k: "Role", v: "Senior Manager, UX Design" },
      { k: "Scope", v: "Product direction, Flows → Journeys → Playbooks" },
      { k: "Status", v: "Journeys shipped · Playbooks conceptual" },
      { k: "Platform", v: "Darwinbox HCM" },
    ]}
    video="/images/work/beyond-the-task/hero-illustration"
    poster="/images/work/beyond-the-task/hero-illustration-poster.jpg"
    alt="Flows, Journeys and AI Playbooks shown as one evolving system — conceptual illustration"
  />

  <ConfidentialityNotice note="My work on Flows, Journeys and AI Playbooks is based on live Darwinbox product work. To respect confidentiality and non-disclosure obligations around proprietary customer data, live interfaces and in-development AI capabilities, actual product screens and specific implementation details have been omitted or replaced with conceptual illustrations. This case study focuses on product strategy, design decisions and publicly communicable direction." />

  <article class="container-pg r-case">
    <!-- Context -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Context</p>
      <h2>Flows already existed. The opportunity was in the experience around them.</h2>
      <p>
        Darwinbox had Flows: transactional automation that moved a request
        through steps — approvals, notifications, data updates. Flows worked,
        but they were isolated. An employee going through something like an
        offboarding or a transfer would hit a series of disconnected tasks,
        each one correct on its own, with nothing connecting them into a
        single, legible experience.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Complexity -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Complexity</p>
      <h2>Why this was hard.</h2>
      <p>
        A guided experience needs to work for two very different people at
        once: the admin, who has to configure something flexible enough to
        cover many scenarios and many customer-specific rules, and the
        employee, who should never see that configuration complexity —
        only a clear next step. Layer AI on top of that and the difficulty
        compounds: the system has to decide when it's confident enough to
        propose something automatically, and the admin has to stay able to
        trust and override every step of what it proposes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem framing -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Problem framing</p>
      <h2>The question changed twice.</h2>
      <p>
        The first version of the problem was: how can we make guided
        experiences useful across many kinds of workflows? That became
        Journeys — a guided experience for the employee, configurable by the
        admin, built on top of the existing Flows.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second version of the problem came later, once Journeys existed:
        an admin still has to manually build every Journey from scratch, even
        though the system already has the employee data, the system events
        and the patterns that would let it suggest one. The question changed
        from <em>"how can an admin create a better Journey?"</em> to
        <em>"why should the admin have to manually create every Journey?"</em>
        That reframing is what Playbooks responds to.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Key design decisions -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Key design decisions</p>
      <h2>The decision that shaped Playbooks.</h2>

      <div class="r-decisions">
        <DecisionBlock
          number="1"
          problem="Once the system could recognize a signal or moment (a role change, a location transfer, a new joiner) well enough to suggest a Playbook, how much of the resulting experience should the AI control versus the admin?"
          options={[
            "AI assembles and activates the Playbook directly, admin reviews after the fact.",
            "AI recommends and proposes steps; admin reviews, edits and approves before anything is assigned.",
          ]}
          decision="AI proposes; the admin remains responsible for reviewing, editing and approving before anything ships to an employee."
          why="An HCM Playbook touches real employee experiences and, often, compliance-sensitive steps. The admin needs to stay the accountable party — AI should remove the blank-page problem of starting from scratch, not remove the admin's judgment from a decision that affects real people."
          tradeoffs="Slower time-to-activation than a fully automated path, and the UI has to carry the extra weight of a clear review/edit/approve step rather than a single 'accept' action."
          result="This human-in-the-loop model is the current design direction for Playbooks. Production status beyond the design/prototype stage still needs confirming — see the note below."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- System: the evolution model -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">System</p>
      <h2>The evolution, as a model.</h2>
      <div class="r-evolution">
        <div class="r-evo-step">
          <p class="r-evo-n">Flows</p>
          <p class="r-evo-d">Transactional automation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="r-evo-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div>
        <div class="r-evo-step">
          <p class="r-evo-n">Journeys</p>
          <p class="r-evo-d">A guided employee experience, built on top of Flows.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="r-evo-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div>
        <div class="r-evo-step">
          <p class="r-evo-n">Playbooks</p>
          <p class="r-evo-d">AI recognizes a signal, recommends a Journey — the admin still decides.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="r-evo-chain-label">The emerging interaction model for Playbooks:</p>
      <SignalChain />
    </section>

    <!-- Craft -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Craft</p>
      <h2>The work.</h2>
      <p>
        For admins, Journeys is a configuration surface — choosing which
        steps, guidance and resources make up a given process. For
        employees, it's a single guided view: what to do, and why it
        matters, instead of a disconnected task list.
      </p>
      <ContentNeeded note="Actual admin configuration and employee-facing Journey screens, plus the AI Playbooks concept/agent-interaction flow, are withheld per the confidentiality notice above — Darwinbox's live interfaces and proprietary customer data can't be shown here. The hero diagram is the one illustration used, and it's explicitly labeled conceptual rather than a real product screen. If a sanitized or genericized set of screens is ever approved for external use, swap them in here." />
    </section>

    <!-- Collaboration -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Collaboration</p>
      <h2>How this got built.</h2>
      <p>
        Led as Senior Manager, UX Design, working with product leadership to
        define the Journeys → Playbooks direction, alongside product and
        engineering partners responsible for the underlying Flows engine and
        the AI recommendation layer.
      </p>
      <ContentNeeded note="Team size, specific collaborators, and timeline weren't present as concrete facts in the source page — add if you want this section more specific than the role-level description above." />
    </section>

    <!-- Outcome -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Outcome</p>
      <h2>What shipped, what was explored.</h2>
      <p>
        Journeys is released and in use by clients. AI Playbooks exists as
        concept and prototype work built around the admin-approval decision
        above; it has not been presented here as a shipped, measured
        product.
      </p>
      <ContentNeeded note="No usage data, adoption numbers or client feedback for Journeys were present in the source page — add real evidence here (adoption, client quotes, or a status update on Playbooks' current rollout) before publishing. Do not state or imply usage numbers without a verified source." />
    </section>

    <!-- Reflection -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Reflection</p>
      <h2>What this work was really about.</h2>
      <p>
        The interesting part of this project wasn't the UI for Journeys —
        it was the shift in the question itself, from "how do we help an
        admin build something" to "why does the admin have to build it at
        all." That's the same shift AI-native product design keeps forcing:
        less time spent designing the configuration screen, more time
        deciding how much judgment a system can responsibly take on, and how
        it stays answerable to the person who's still accountable for the
        outcome.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .r-case { max-width: 46rem; padding: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem) 0 clamp(4rem, 8vw, 6rem); }
  .r-case-section { margin-bottom: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem); }
  .r-case-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-ink-3);
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }
  .r-case-section h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(1.375rem, 2.6vw, 1.875rem); letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .r-case-section p {
    font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-ink-2); margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .r-case-section em { color: var(--color-ink); font-style: italic; }

  .r-decisions { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.5rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; }

  .r-evolution {
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: 0.5rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;
  }
  .r-evo-step {
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem; border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 0.75rem;
    width: 100%;
  }
  .r-evo-n { font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 0.25rem; }
  .r-evo-d { font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); margin: 0; }
  .r-evo-arrow { color: var(--color-ink-3); padding-left: 1.25rem; }

  .r-evo-chain-label { font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--color-ink-3); margin: 0 0 0.75rem; }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .r-evolution { flex-direction: row; align-items: stretch; }
    .r-evo-step { flex: 1; }
    .r-evo-arrow { display: flex; align-items: center; padding: 0 0.25rem; transform: rotate(-90deg); }
  }
</style>
